#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107037 :tutorial/content "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(xy)^{4} &=  \\\\\n(xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) &=  \\\\\n(x \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot x)(y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{4}y^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "7aabbc0f-a3ca-5f05-b6ae-d7551b16ae1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
